## 5. Memory Management

The operating system manages memory and prevents processes from accessing memory they are not allowed to use.

### Virtual Memory

**Virtual Memory** provides each process with its own virtual address space.

A process behaves as if it has its own private memory, even though physical RAM is shared between processes.

### Virtual Memory vs. Physical Memory

| Virtual Memory | Physical Memory |
|---|---|
| Addresses used by a process | Actual RAM locations |
| Provides a private address space | Shared physical hardware |
| Managed through address translation | Real memory |

### Memory Management Unit (MMU)

The **Memory Management Unit (MMU)** is hardware that translates virtual memory addresses into physical memory addresses.

The operating system and MMU work together to enforce memory protection.

### Memory Protection

Memory protection helps prevent:
- One process accessing another process's memory.
- User applications accessing kernel memory.
- Unauthorized modification of memory.
- Programs accessing invalid memory locations.

### Segmentation Fault

A **Segmentation Fault** occurs when a program attempts to access memory that it is not permitted to access or that has no valid mapping.

This is a protection mechanism that helps prevent applications from accessing invalid memory.

