## 24. DMA – Direct Memory Access

**DMA (Direct Memory Access)** allows certain hardware devices to transfer data directly to or from system memory without the CPU handling every individual transfer.

DMA improves performance, but it also creates security concerns because a DMA-capable device may potentially access memory outside the normal CPU-mediated access-control path.

### Why DMA Attacks are Dangerous

Normal OS permissions primarily control CPU-mediated access.

A DMA-capable device may potentially access physical memory through the hardware I/O path. Without suitable hardware and OS protections, this access may not be subject to the same user-account and file-permission checks.

Therefore:
- User accounts do not by themselves prevent DMA access.
- Filesystem permissions do not directly restrict DMA access to physical memory.
- Disk encryption does not necessarily protect data that is already accessible in RAM.

### IOMMU

An **IOMMU (Input-Output Memory Management Unit)** provides address translation and isolation for devices that perform DMA.

It can restrict devices to memory regions they are allowed to access.

### Defense Against DMA Attacks

- Enable Kernel DMA Protection when supported.
- Use IOMMU-based protections.
- Enable appropriate firmware security settings.
- Restrict or disable unused DMA-capable interfaces.
- Use physical security.
- Avoid leaving devices unattended while powered on.
- Keep firmware and OS updated.
- Use hardware with stronger security features.

### Thunderspy

Thunderspy was research into weaknesses in Thunderbolt security, demonstrating that physical access and vulnerable device-security mechanisms can create serious risks.

The research highlighted why DMA protections and hardware/firmware security matter.

Spycheck was developed to help users assess their systems and provide protection recommendations.

